WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.710
Two students each asked forty classmates

2
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.130
how long they spend on homework.

3
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.100
One found fifty minutes and

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.380
the other found sixty.

5
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.590
Was one of them wrong?

6
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.920
Today: why a sample never gives exactly

7
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.520
the right answer, and how wrong it might

8
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:17.800
be.

9
00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.470
The population is everyone we care

10
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.870
about, say all eight hundred pupils.

11
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.340
A sample is the group we actually ask.

12
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.810
We use the sample to estimate

13
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.550
something about the whole population.

14
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.820
Pick forty pupils at random

15
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.640
and you get one average.

16
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.470
Pick another forty and you get a

17
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.490
slightly different one.

18
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.540
Neither student was wrong,

19
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.980
because every random sample

20
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.180
wobbles around the true value.

21
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.130
We took twenty random samples from the

22
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.570
school records and plotted their averages.

23
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.080
Most land between fifty and sixty minutes,

24
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.140
around the true value of fifty five.

25
00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.610
So we report a range, not one number.

26
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.430
We say fifty five minutes,

27
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:07.850
plus or minus five.

28
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.180
A bigger sample shrinks the margin, but

29
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.980
four times as many pupils only halves it.

30
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.000
A big sample can still be

31
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.040
wrong if it is not random.

32
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.710
If we only ask pupils who stay after

33
00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.790
school, we miss those who leave early to

34
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:26.090
work.

35
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.460
Always ask who was left out.

36
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:31.380
Quick check!

37
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.170
Two random samples of forty pupils

38
00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.690
give fifty and sixty minutes.

39
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.580
What is the best explanation?

40
00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.610
Random samples vary, so both

41
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.490
answers are reasonable.

42
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.560
Together they suggest the true value

43
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.380
is somewhere near fifty five.

44
00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.250
A range tells that story honestly.

45
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.800
In the app, open the sampling lab.

46
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.200
Draw many samples, then make each

47
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.300
sample bigger, and watch the

48
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.300
averages crowd closer together.

49
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:07.030
Your turn.

50
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.260
We want a smaller margin of error.

51
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:11.720
What should we do?

52
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.010
Ask more pupils, chosen at random.

53
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.910
Remember: samples wobble, so report a

54
00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.590
range, and check who your sample left out.

55
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.550
Try it yourself: put numbered

56
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.630
cards in a bag, draw ten at a

57
00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.550
time, and work out each average.

58
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.140
Why are your averages all

59
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:31.780
a little different?
